cmtm5udne003nzlil2zq86of5	The Guardian view on Nigeria’s kidnappings: a country can’t prosper when going to school is a danger | Editorial	Abductions are resurging and may be spreading to new areas – with young children among the victims. A military response alone won’t work\nEarly last month, Nigeria’s government announced the largest single-day hostage rescue in its history, with the freeing of 300 people who had been taken in raids in two north central states. Celebrations were short-lived. Barely a fortnight later, gunmen abducted up to 600 worshippers from a mosque in Niger state. Nigerians paid kidnappers nearly $6m in ransoms in the year to June, according to a new report from the Lagos-based consultancy SBM Intelligence – more than triple the total in the previous 12-month period. More than 7,800 people were abducted, a two-thirds increase, and more than 1,100 killed, including almost 900 civilians.\nWhen Boko Haram abducted almost 300 schoolgirls from their school in Chibok, Borno state in 2014, it made headlines worldwide. Yet around 45 children, mostly aged between two and five, remain missing after they were snatched from schools in Mussa, in north-eastern Borno state, in May. There is growing concern that kidnappings may be spreading to new areas. Forty-six students and staff were abducted from three schools in the south-western state of Oyo in the same month; they were rescued in July.\nDo you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our letters section, please click here.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/03/the-guardian-view-on-nigerias-kidnappings-a-country-cant-prosper-when-going-to-school-is-a-danger	\N	UK	2026-09-03 17:44:44	2026-09-03 23:32:49.178	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udnh003ozlil9pp4hnbp	Climate injustices have been laid bare in the Nepal-Tibet flood disaster | Letters	Prof Lyla Mehta says the glacial floods and loss of life are a stark reminder that climate disruption is global and highly unequal. Plus letters from Lynda McDonald and Jan Bengtsson\nThe recent glacial collapse and catastrophic floods in Nepal and Tibet are not isolated tragedies (Report, 28 August). They reflect accelerating climate disruption that disproportionately affects the countries and people that are least responsible for global emissions.\nAs climate change intensifies, glaciers around the world, including in the Himalayas, are melting at unprecedented rates, destabilising ecosystems and causing glacial collapse and ice-and-rock landslides, destroying settlements, villages and livelihoods in minutes. No one died when a Swiss glacier collapsed last year, owing to early-warning systems that were in place. But a lack of early-warning systems means that the disaster in Nepal and Tibet has led to hundreds of deaths and thousands missing.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/03/climate-injustices-have-been-laid-bare-in-the-nepal-tibet-flood-disaster	\N	UK	2026-09-03 16:52:29	2026-09-03 23:32:49.181	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
