cmtm5udoz004nzlildgf5qrsd	Bucking Fastard review – Herzog’s starry twins soap provokes a syncing feeling	Venice film festival: Rooney and Kate Mara play the real-life Chaplin sisters who spoke in unsettling unison in this silly, pointless drama\nWerner Herzog is such a unique film-maker that even a dud from him has some interest, but sadly a dud is what this is. It is a fiction feature based on a true story which – despite a few laughs – systematically wastes the audience’s time, as well as the talents of its formidable female leads, real-life sisters Rooney and Kate Mara. You can almost feel this pointless, aimless film making it up as it goes along, losing its way, line by line, scene by scene.\nIt is inspired by the story of twins Freda and Greta Chaplin from York in the UK; they eerily spoke in unison and in the 1980s were the subject of a highly publicised court case for harassing a neighbour. Herzog’s movie transplants their story to Ireland, in which the Maras play weirdly but comically interdependent sisters Jean and Joan Holbrook whose lives are sent in all sorts of loopy directions. Orlando Bloom plays the roguish neighbour who breaks the women’s hearts (Herzog imagines them as a short-lived throuple), and Domhnall Gleeson is a caring social worker who tries to make them less dysfunctional by taking their case after they move away from the scene of their sexual resentment and legal shame. (During the trial they shout out the spoonerism of the title.)\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/03/bucking-fastard-review-werner-herzog-rooney-kate-mara-chaplin-sisters	\N	UK	2026-09-03 15:00:41	2026-09-03 23:32:49.235	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp0004ozlilatkxbldl	By Any Means review – Mark Wahlberg and Yahya Abdul-Mateen II drama provokes with horror of 1960s racism	Elegance Bratton’s civil rights drama in the Jim Crow south confronts you with almost unbearable brutality\n\n\nBlack people officially won the unrestricted right to vote in the US in 1965 but many still encountered hurdles on their path to the ballot box: 11 states in the south quickly introduced a poll tax requiring an annual fee to be allowed to vote. The practice unambiguously targeted poor Black communities in an early form of thinly veiled racist voter suppression. Vernon Dahmer wouldn’t stand for it. In January 1966 the Hattiesburg, Mississippi-based Black activist and NAACP chapter head promised to cover the fee for those struggling to afford it. “It’s a proclamation that we here, that we matter,” he says in an early scene in By Any Means, a considered, if blunt, drama inspired by the real-life aftermath of Dahmer’s murder by the Ku Klux Klan.\nIt is Mississippi in the mid-60s and Wayne Strider (a sturdy Yahya Abdul-Mateen II) is new to the local FBI unit, having recently moved back to his home state from Chicago, and is dealing with bosses who turn a blind eye to racist crimes. “White guys can get away with murder,” says one Black colleague while shaking his head. After his boss receives a message from President Johnson to prioritize the Dahmer murder case, Strider is teamed up with a widely feared mobster, Gregory Scarpa (Mark Wahlberg with a rather distracting prosthetic nose), who has been shipped in from Brooklyn as part of an off-book mission to track down the Klansmen and bring justice to the killers.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/03/by-any-means-review-mark-wahlberg-yahya-abdul-mateen-ii-elegance-bratton	\N	UK	2026-09-03 16:00:42	2026-09-03 23:32:49.236	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp1004pzlilra85grl8	La Mesías review – this utterly bizarre TV show is unforgettable	Finally landing on Mubi, this Spanish mega-hit about six sisters drilled into becoming viral Christian pop stars is wildly ambitious, blackly funny and a true triumph. Oh, and there are UFOs too\nAt Primavera Sound festival in Barcelona in 2024, nestled alongside the likes of Pulp and Charli xcx was an unusual addition to the bill. Stella Maris arrived on stage as haunting home movies, religious iconography and glittery gifs popped up on the screen behind them. Part-Haim, part-Heaven’s Gate, the six sisters performed their hits: “Christian electronic dance music” that their late mother, apparently, believed would help them to save the world.\nThe performance was very real, but the band was not. It was a stunt for the Spanish TV mega-hit La Mesías or The Messiah, which was released there in 2023. Its seven episodes centre on an estranged brother and sister, Enric and Irene, who discover that their siblings have become a viral pop band. Cue a soft-focus, flashback-heavy trip into the family’s past, thick with emotional neglect, parental narcissism and religious fanaticism. Oh, and there are UFOs, too.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/sep/03/la-mesias-review-mubi	\N	UK	2026-09-03 12:00:25	2026-09-03 23:32:49.237	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp3004qzlilxdeohnnw	Chad Powers season two review – Glen Powell’s sweet sports comedy scores a touchdown	The Hollywood hottie is back as the quarterback in disguise, in an oddball comedy that is naughty, lovable and disarmingly unique\nIn the first episode of season two of Chad Powers, there is but one concern: the main character’s nose has fallen off.\nThe TV outlet for the wacky side of movie hottie Glen Powell, Chad Powers presents itself to us having willingly taken on the burden of a ludicrous premise. Powell is Russ Holliday, a former hotshot college-football quarterback who, eight years ago, committed a career-cancelling faux pas during the televised game that was meant to make him a star. With his sporting dreams suddenly vaporised, he became an obnoxious playboy. But then, just as he felt his feet starting to scrape rock bottom, he heard that a university in Georgia was holding open tryouts for a new quarterback. Donning prosthetic whiskers, teeth, hair and an occasionally unreliable nose – a slightly more sophisticated version of a joke-shop disguise – Russ became “Chad Powers”, a southern oddball and the new star of the team, while constantly dealing with the threat of being found out and sent home.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/sep/03/chad-powers-season-two-review-disney-plus-glen-powell	\N	UK	2026-09-03 09:00:48	2026-09-03 23:32:49.239	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp4004rzlilpgk2ihbq	The Gesualdo Six/Colin Currie Group review – surely the year’s most atmospheric Prom	Royal Albert Hall, London\nThe Gesualdo Six brought focus and luminous tone to a sequence of early devotional music; Reich’s Tehillim made a mesmeric and ecstatic pairing\nWhen Steve Reich wrote Tehillim, half his long life ago, it was a very modern contribution to the ancient tradition of psalm singing. In this late-night Prom celebrating Reich’s 90th birthday, Tehillim was presented next to a sequence of devotional music from medieval Europe, making for what was surely one of this year’s most atmospheric Proms.\nFor the first half the stage was held by the singers of the Gesualdo Six, who brought intense focus, luminous tone and consistently outstanding vocal quality to polyphonic works by Machaut, Ockeghem, Dunstable and Josquin des Prez, all sung from memory, interspersed with solo melodies by Pérotin and Hildegard of Bingen. Odd one out, in theory, was a version of Hail Gladdening Light written in 2017 by Owain Park, the Gesualdo Six’s bass and director; but this brief chant-like solo for countertenor, with harmonies filled in by the rest of the singers’ humming, fitted in almost seamlessly.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/03/the-gesualdo-six-colin-currie-group-steve-reich-proms-review	\N	UK	2026-09-03 17:21:25	2026-09-03 23:32:49.24	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp6004szlil7i23p8fz	Adéla: Prima review – Slovakian star smashes the fourth wall to bask in pop’s cynicism and seduction	(Polydor)\nThe 22-year-old is impressively frank about indulging industry sleaze in her pursuit of fame on a debut that puts a distinctive spin on the best of 00s hedonism\nThere’s something very striking about hearing a pop star in 2026, their voice larded with Auto-Tune, opening a song with the line “I can’t really sing”. We live in an era where fans seem more aware than ever of the nefarious music business machinations that lurk within the star-making process, one where you’re never far away from online outrage decrying some artist or other as an industry plant. Nevertheless, admitting that the ubiquitous audio processing software is sometimes deployed for a helping hand, not a futuristic aesthetic, feels strangely jarring. On one hand, well of course it is. On the other, actually announcing that without it, you might be honking like a despondent goose, in the first line of a song, still amounts to a dramatic breaking of the fourth wall.\nThat said, it’s very much on brand for Adéla Jergová, who started her career as a contestant on a TV talent show – she failed to make the cut for crumbling “global girl group” Katseye, as documented on Netflix’s Pop Star Academy – before established names alighted on her as a more left-field solo proposition: Grimes co-produced her early single Machine Girl. New artists are well advised to have a USP, something that helps them cut through the mass of peers vying for public attention on streaming services. The 22-year-old Slovakian has chosen to differentiate herself from the serried ranks of post-Brat female pop stars by spiking her songs about hedonism and messy sexual encounters (“no bed frame, we can do it on the carpet … you eat real good for a starving artist”) with a kind of meta commentary on what it is to be a female pop star.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/03/adela-prima-review-polydor	\N	UK	2026-09-03 14:30:40	2026-09-03 23:32:49.242	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp7004tzlilh9p316nk	‘I want to go further’: Justin Broadrick on retiring Godflesh, playing with Phoebe Bridgers – and his new band	A hernia may mean his screaming days are over, but the Napalm Death alum’s influence on metal echoes on in unexpected places – and with ‘heavy, ugly’ experiments in the pipeline, his passion for ‘outsider music’ remains strong\nOn 4 October 2024, Justin Broadrick felt a pop. The singer and guitarist of pioneering industrial metal duo Godflesh had felt pain in his groin for several years. After a coughing fit following a show in Sofia, Bulgaria, he knew he had a hernia. “It was a small knob in my groin,” says Broadrick. “I got back home and told my partner, and she was like, ‘Let’s watch how it goes.’” He continued to perform live for another year, wearing up to two hernia belts at a time. By the time he got it surgically treated in February this year, it was 13cm long.\nThe next month, Broadrick shared a picture of himself in hospital and announced that Godflesh’s near-four-decade career will soon end. His condition had been caused by the physicality of shouting over their music since he was 18; he just turned 57. If he were to do so again, he would risk blowing out his abdominal wall entirely. There will be two more studio albums – starting with this autumn’s reliably crushing Decay, recorded pre-surgery – and possibly some archival releases and remixes. But beyond that? Nothing.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/03/justin-broadrick-godflesh-phoebe-bridgers	\N	UK	2026-09-03 15:00:45	2026-09-03 23:32:49.243	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp8004uzlillzrdds7e	Berlin Philharmonic/Petrenko review – insightful Elgar and Tchaikovsky holds Proms audience in thrall	Royal Albert Hall, London\nElgar’s Enigma Variations sounded fresh and striking in the German orchestra’s reading, while Tchaikovsky’s fourth symphony was thrilling and monumental\nIs the Berlin Philharmonic the best orchestra in the world? Quite possibly. The only potential disappointment about the first of their two Proms under their chief conductor Kirill Petrenko was that there were just two works. Elgar’s Enigma Variations and Tchaikovsky’s Symphony No 4 made an insightful pairing, though, sharing an autobiographical aspect that, in different ways, reveals much about their composers.\nThe Elgar – bread-and-butter repertoire for UK orchestras but not yet for German ones – was approached with a freshness that made these portraits of the composer’s friends and family seem particularly affectionate. The opening can sound like merely a warm-up for what’s to come; here, though, from the very start the string tone was sumptuous and pregnant with possibility, the phrasing meaningful. This attention to detail continued throughout a performance that gloried in the richness of the orchestra’s sound and the full extent of its range, from the softest of melancholy cello solos to the floor-shaking throb of the Royal Albert Hall organ in the finale. A particularly striking moment came with the stark timpani rolls in the penultimate variation, conjuring the sound of a ship’s engine to honour a friend who was then on a long sea voyage – or, reading deeper, perhaps a lost love who had emigrated years before.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/03/berlin-philharmonic-petrenko-review-proms-elgar-tchaikovsky	\N	UK	2026-09-03 14:31:07	2026-09-03 23:32:49.244	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udp9004vzlili3en71es	What we’re reading: Irvine Welsh, Janice Hallett and Guardian readers on the books they enjoyed in August	Writers and Guardian readers discuss the titles they have read over the last month. Join the conversation in the comments\nI’ve been enjoying Contrapposto by Dave Eggers. The premise that love and art are the only things that make life worthwhile pulses beautifully through this novel and its two protagonists, spanning decades of their obsession with both, and each other. The tragedy is they are not sufficient alone for a relationship to survive to the exclusion of everything else (namely capitalism, which always has a place at the table), but they remain essential and enduring. Nowhere is this better evoked than here. Think John Williams’s Stoner but with art instead of literature and stuffed with more memorable characters.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/03/what-were-reading-irvine-welsh-janice-hallett-and-guardian-readers-on-the-books-they-enjoyed-in-august	\N	UK	2026-09-03 12:01:06	2026-09-03 23:32:49.245	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpa004wzlilyct1woik	A Death of One’s Own by Terry Eagleton review – meditations on arriving at a full stop	The literary critic brings his vast reading to bear on life’s most inescapable fact\nTerry Eagleton is not a fan of sloppy thinking, especially when it comes to the granite fact of death. He particularly doesn’t like the attitude of Dylan Thomas who famously urged his ailing father to “not go gentle into that good night”. If the “night” – death – is so “good” then why, asks Eagleton reasonably, would Thomas want his dad to dawdle? Shouldn’t DJ Thomas, rather, be encouraged to trot towards his lovely end like an ecstatic marathon runner on the home straight?\nEagleton’s reproach to Thomas’s doublethink should not be read as panicky denial about the reality of his own mortality. To the contrary, Eagleton, who at 83 admits that he wasn’t sure whether this book would end up being published posthumously, finds it hard to think or write about much else. After a 60-year academic career spent tussling with Marxist literary theory, not to mention the epistemological void at the heart of postmodernism criticism, these days Eagleton is more interested in pondering death and how it “lacks a sense of occasion”. It might visit, for example, while you happen to be dressed in a giraffe outfit.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/03/a-death-of-ones-own-by-terry-eagleton-review-meditations-on-arriving-at-a-full-stop	\N	UK	2026-09-03 06:00:45	2026-09-03 23:32:49.246	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpb004xzliltqa240av	Chasing Freedom: a poignant reflection on empire, education and parental expectations	Simukai Chigudu’s filmic memoir details the confines and conflicts of growing up in Zimbabwe during political turmoil.\n\nDon’t already get The Long Wave in your inbox? Sign up here\n\n\nLast week, I finally managed to snatch one read out of the jaws of a hectic summer. On a series of very long-haul flights (highly recommended as a way to break reading droughts), I fell into the world of Simukai Chigudu’s Chasing Freedom: Coming of Age at the End of Empire. I finished the book just as I was landing in Nairobi, Kenya. In a daze, I stepped on to the tarmac in the chill of equatorial winter.\nThe book was about growing up in post-colonial Zimbabwe and navigating life between the ex-colonised and the ex-colonial power. But the account of Chigudu’s life also captured, in ways that sometimes left me breathless with the shock of recognition, what felt like a broader shared experience for those of us born to parents whose traumas we never understood. This week, I reflected on how Chigudu’s experience connects to that of a wider post-colonial African generation.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/news/2026/sep/02/chasing-freedom-simukai-chigudu-empire-parental-expectations	\N	UK	2026-09-02 13:42:35	2026-09-03 23:32:49.247	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpc004yzlil1cituu3s	The Bridge by Colm Tóibín review – betrayal and survival in the High Pyrenees	Deep sorrow mingles with illicit desire in this potent novella, the follow-up to a 2006 story about an isolated family in the Catalan mountains\nTwenty years ago, Colm Tóibín published a steadily desolating novella set in the high villages of the Catalan Pyrenees. A woman walks out of her house and her life, starts along the mountain road and disappears in the snow. Her son Miquel, failing to find her body, waits through the dark months for the thaw. A Long Winter, which appeared in a standalone volume and then as the final story in the 2006 collection Mothers and Sons, followed Miquel through dramatically extreme circumstances and patient endurance. It was told with the most restrained prose ever likely to be employed for a son’s view of vultures circling a corpse.\nNow, in The Bridge, a single year has passed in Miquel’s life and he is eking out an existence with his hurtful, unyielding father. The sheep are grazing their high summer pastures; a side hustle smuggling contraband brings in a little much-needed cash. In the bare conditions of the lonely farm, any warmth of feeling is a kind of contraband smuggled in by night; love between men is doubly illicit. The grave tenderness that grows between Miquel and his father’s maltreated servant Manolo gives The Bridge its emotional centre. These young men salve the emptiness with a few quiet words and touches. We sense their youthful beauty and resilience, their suppleness in a place of fixity.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/02/the-bridge-by-colm-toibin-review-betrayal-and-survival-in-the-high-pyrenees	\N	UK	2026-09-02 06:00:24	2026-09-03 23:32:49.248	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
