cmtm5udpe0050zlil556ghpn2	Grand Theft Auto VI is a phenomenon – but there is much more to the gaming world	As Rockstar’s blockbuster prepares to dominate the headlines, it’s time for the mainstream media to stop treating video games as a novelty that needs explaining\nPredictably, the Grand Theft Auto VI “extended look” which premiered on Netflix last week was a success for all involved. The 30-minute preview scorched to No 1 on the viewing chart and even brought the site down for a few minutes as millions of gamers tried to watch what was, effectively, a very long teaser trailer. According to data collated by Eurogamer, more than 9,000 Twitch streamers covered the show leading to 5,227,798 hours of viewing there alone. And this was just the beginning of what will surely be two months of escalating hype – and unprecedented media attention.\nCall me cynical, but I suspect the coverage of GTA VI in the media over the next few weeks will follow a familiar pattern. Mostly, it will be five-minute slots wedged in near the end of news bulletins in which games journalists, industry analysts and other pundits will be asked why the game is such a big deal, how much it cost to make and how much revenue it will generate. Perhaps a few will try to reignite the hoary old “violence in video games” debate, there will be presenters laughingly admitting they’ve not touched a game since Pac-Man, rolling their eyes and wallowing in their ignorance, in a way they never would while discussing any other art form. Somebody will venture the subversive view that “games are no longer just for kids”; somebody else will say “games make more money than films and music put together”. And that will be that, job done for another decade … or until there’s a funny story about Pokémon.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/games/2026/sep/02/pushing-buttons-newsletter-grand-theft-auto-vi-cultural-phenomenon	\N	UK	2026-09-02 14:00:26	2026-09-03 23:32:49.25	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{games,culture,"grand theft auto","grand theft auto vi",netflix,"film adaptations",twitch}
cmtm5udpj0053zlilhst351k6	‘I was never taught Welsh history’: Michael Sheen on reclaiming rebel leader Owain Glyndŵr	Actor will play 15th-century hero in sweeping production about Welsh rebellion against English crown\nThere are myriad films, plays and books focusing on heroes of English, Scottish and Irish history but far fewer that tell the stories of the great figures of Wales.\nA major new play about the Welsh nobleman Owain Glyndŵr’s early 15th-century rebellion against the English crown will help redress the balance this autumn.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2026/sep/03/michael-sheen-on-playing-owain-glyndwr-wales	\N	UK	2026-09-03 06:17:16	2026-09-03 23:32:49.255	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"michael sheen",wales,stage,culture,heritage,theatre,cardiff,"uk news",acting}
cmtm5udpk0054zlil9apvf9mi	‘Finally, a victory for the middle-aged white man!’ Chris Cantrill on winning the Edinburgh comedy award	The ex-marketing manager’s win, for his spoof Sycamore Gap-inspired whodunnit Rewilding, arrives just as he’s gearing up for a new season of hit sketch show SNL UK – which he calls ‘the hardest job of my life’\nFor the last month, Chris Cantrill has been performing his fringe show Rewilding daily in a lunchtime slot. And yet, when we plan to speak about his victory in the Edinburgh comedy awards, the interview is arranged – on the festival’s penultimate day – for lunchtime. Can he really have won the fringe’s major prize, and – hell of a mic drop, this – immediately sacked off his last two shows? “I couldn’t be happier not to be at the festival today,” he says, on video call from his Northumberland home. But the cancellation, it transpires, is long-standing, because work starts immediately post-fringe on the new series of SNL UK, for which he writes.\n“But I’m absolutely over the moon not to be doing my show right now,” he admits. “I’ve got a lot of adrenaline in my body slowly seeping out, and I feel like I’ve been hit by a train.” The Edinburgh comedy award (it used to be the Perrier, and was won in the past by Steve Coogan, The League of Gentlemen and Frank Skinner) has been a long time coming for Bradford-born Cantrill. This year’s was his fourth nomination, twice as a solo act, and twice as part of his duo The Delightful Sausage, alongside 2024 winner Amy Gledhill. He is, as I tease him when we speak, a comparatively senior winner, the oldest since Bridget Christie, also 42, 13 years ago. “Finally, a victory for the middle-aged white man!,” he retorts, then bursts into laughter. “Common sense at last!”\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/stage/2026/sep/03/chris-cantrill-edinburgh-comedy-award-snl-uk-rewilding-sycamore-gap	\N	UK	2026-09-03 11:00:04	2026-09-03 23:32:49.256	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"edinburgh festival 2026",comedy,comedy,culture,"edinburgh festival",stage}
cmtm5udpn0056zlilgrh1mxgl	Ready tae Drap review – clashing neighbours trapped in a lift get roars of approval	Òran Mór, Glasgow\nComedy chemistry drives a play that asks if three contrasting characters – conspiracy theorist, pregnant mum, hen-do attendee – can find common ground\nTrapping three nightmare neighbours in the dodgy lift of their Glasgow high-rise, Ready tae Drap has lots of sparky, slapstick humour – but not so much nuance.\nCrotchety conspiracy theorist Pat (Isabelle Joss) is in her 70s and armed with barbed, uninvited observations. Saintly, exhausted Muslim mum of two Aaliyah (Tinashe Warikandwa) is on the verge of giving birth to her third child. Dry-humoured Lauren (performed by playwright Éimi Quinn) is the play’s millennial, fourth-wall-breaking narrator, clad in fishnets and bunny ears for a hen-do. It’s a classic setup: can these contrasting characters find common ground in a tight spot?\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/stage/2026/sep/02/ready-tae-drap-review-oran-mor-glasgow	\N	UK	2026-09-02 11:46:55	2026-09-03 23:32:49.259	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpo0057zlilfafzfspy	Martin McDonagh says CIA has ‘done awful things all around the world’	McDonagh’s new film, Wild Horse Nine, stars John Malkovich as an agent in 1970s Chile who feels ‘guilt and regret’ over his role in global interventions\nThe US’s history of intervening in democratic processes around the world deserves more attention in the light of current political developments, playwright and film-maker Martin McDonagh has said, as his Oscar-tipped film about a rueful CIA agent premieres at the Venice film festival.\n“I’ve been very interested in the truth of what the CIA has been doing over the last 60 years, and the awful things they’ve done all around the world,” McDonagh said about Wild Horse Nine, his first film since his 2022 tragicomedy The Banshees of Inisherin. “I think maybe something that’s not talked about enough. We know what the KGB are like, but we probably don’t question the CIA’s activity as much as we ought to.”\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/03/martin-mcdonagh-wild-horse-nine-cia-awful-things	\N	UK	2026-09-03 14:21:51	2026-09-03 23:32:49.26	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpp0058zlilvem1neti	Margaret Preston: Australia’s most beloved and contested artist?	The Ian Potter Centre: NGV Australia has staged the first Preston retrospective in 20 years, exploring what was gained and flattened in her quest to create a singular Australian aesthetic\nHanging at the entrance of the new Margaret Preston exhibition at the National Gallery of Victoria are three of her instantly recognisable still lifes. Each depicts a vase brimming with native flora: bell-shaped gumnuts, silvery eucalyptus leaves and bristly banksias. These are identifiably Australian plants, painted in what might be described as an Australian style, reflecting Preston’s lifelong ambition to define a national aesthetic. Look more closely, though, and this simplistic story begins to unravel.\nHer 1928 painting Western Australian Gum Blossom features a blocky background of creamy rectangles that gesture to the modernist and abstract movements that were flourishing in Europe at the time, as well as the flattened forms of Japanese woodblock prints. The black and white geometry of the second work, Native Honeysuckle, echoes cubist paintings by the French artist Fernand Léger. And all three point to Preston’s interest in Indigeneity, a fascination intertwined with her appreciation and appropriation of Aboriginal art.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/ng-interactive/2026/sep/04/australian-painter-margaret-preston-exhibition-ngv	\N	UK	2026-09-03 15:00:43	2026-09-03 23:32:49.261	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpq0059zliliezmi5sm	‘My work has always been serious’: Maurizio Cattelan on his $6m banana, his gold toilet – and bringing Hitler to Berlin	He is famed for creating a golden loo that was stolen and a $6m banana that was eaten. But, says the sculptor ahead of a major new show in Germany, there’s more to his work than pranks and provocation\nMaurizio Cattelan, the art world’s merriest prankster, pauses when asked which object in his sprawling new Berlin show is most likely to be defaced, stolen or eaten, then emits a foghorn of a laugh. “There is no need to vandalise anything,” says the trim, soulful Italian, explaining that he has already taken care of all that, given how the work cuts villains, imposters and straw men down to size. “But,” he says, referring to the show’s more daring works, “maybe we need someone to resuscitate people.”\nCattelan, who speaks in a lilting English, grins broadly, a mischievous glint in his brown eyes. Speaking on the subterranean level of Mies van der Rohe’s weightless Neue Nationalgalerie (NNG) in Berlin, he darts between the impish and the profound in conversation, mirroring the dizzying range of his work. The silver-haired sculptor is best known for duct-taping a banana to a wall in 2019 and calling it Comedian, in a droll commentary on the excesses of the art market. The joke became even funnier when it sold for $6.2m, then hilarious when its buyer took a bite out of his purchase and swallowed it.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2026/sep/03/maurizio-cattelan-art-world-prankster-religion-bananas-hitler	\N	UK	2026-09-03 04:00:42	2026-09-03 23:32:49.262	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpr005azlilqphak1ap	New musical to give intimate portrait of Marilyn Monroe and Arthur Miller’s marriage	Misfits Together, by Bastille’s Dan Smith and To Kill a King’s Ralph Pelleymounter, is set amid the 1950s’ ‘red scare’\nIt was lampooned as the union of the “hourglass” and the “egghead” – the marriage, in the summer of 1956, between Hollywood superstar Marilyn Monroe and Pulitzer-winning playwright Arthur Miller. Now, a new stage musical is to give a nuanced and intimate portrait of that famous relationship, treat each as trailblazers in their own way and reveal parallels between the volatile state of the US during the 50s “red scare” and now.\nThe production, which opens next autumn at Leicester’s Curve, is called Misfits Together, a reference to the film Miller wrote for Monroe that was released shortly after their divorce in 1961. It is a foray into musical theatre by two indie musicians, Dan Smith of the band Bastille and Ralph Pelleymounter from To Kill a King, who have recorded the score as a studio album. The first track, Oh America, is out on Thursday.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/stage/2026/sep/03/dan-smith-ralph-pelleymounter-marilyn-monroe-arthur-miller-misfits-together	\N	UK	2026-09-03 07:00:46	2026-09-03 23:32:49.263	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpx005gzlil6h7giitr	Jess Cartner-Morley’s September style essentials: slipper-soft shoes, miracle hair mist and a worth-the-wait cardigan	Got that new term feeling? Embrace the September reset with tailored blazers, hints of cashmere and a surprisingly excellent tee\n• Don’t get the Filter delivered to your inbox? Sign up here\nNobody has handed me a timetable in decades, but I still love that new-term energy, and this edit leans right into it. A blazer that is properly tailored, rather than assembled from buttons and hope. Closed-toe shoes to ensure a proper soft landing after months of sandals. A T-shirt from a brand I was fully prepared to be sniffy about, until it made me eat my words. And a packing solution, because the only sane way to deal with the end of summer is to plan a holiday, obviously.\nThere’s also a genius lip colour for lip balm addicts like me, a bag doing a very convincing impression of something 10 times its price, and one long-sold-out cardigan that was worth every month of the wait.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/thefilter/2026/sep/01/jess-cartner-morleys-september-style-essentials-2026	\N	UK	2026-09-01 14:00:01	2026-09-03 23:32:49.27	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udpv005ezlildd5jqa7s	The best kettles in the UK to save energy and speed up your cuppa, tested	We boiled litres of water to find the best electric kettles, from hard-water heroes to vintage-style, repairable and wifi-connected models\n• The best air fryers, tried and tested for crisp and crunch\nDespite the march of progress, the humble kettle remains a kitchen staple. It’s what we turn to in times of strife, when spirits are flagging, or to start our day. When a visitor calls, one of the first things we often do is put the kettle on.\nMany small appliances have evolved beyond their original form, but the kettle’s basic principles remain largely unchanged. Water goes in and heats until a thermostat switches it off; the water then pours out, and we enjoy a cuppa. However, the technology that goes into a kettle has been slowly improving: better insulation to keep water hotter for longer and reduce reboils; different temperature settings to suit every drink from green tea to herbal brews; and more features such as filters and concealed elements to keep scale out of our cups.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/thefilter/2025/jul/22/best-electric-kettles-uk	\N	UK	2026-09-02 14:00:08	2026-09-03 23:32:49.267	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{tea,gadgets,coffee,interiors,"life and style",technology,"kitchen essentials"}
cmtm5udpw005fzlilo40kttsb	The best portable neck and handheld fans in the UK to keep you cool, tested	The seasons might be changing, but the muggy weather remains, and heatwaves are on the rise. Stay comfortable on the move with our tried and tested portable neck and handheld fans\n• The best fans for your home, tested\n• Shark ChillPill 3-in-1 fan review\nSo far this year, we’ve seen five heatwaves in the UK. The climate crisis means things are heating up year on year, and while we can hide in air-conditioned shops and offices, our homes can get uncomfortably – sometimes dangerously – hot.\nWe also have to go outside – or worse, pack ourselves like sweaty sardines on sweltering public transport. On those occasions, a portable fan can make all the difference between manageable discomfort and indulging in an inadvisable quantity of ice-cream. Their recent popularity has led to manufacturers churning out cheap-but-not-especially cheerful products that ultimately end up as clutter in your house – or worse, landfill, where an estimated 4.3m will end up this year alone.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/thefilter/2025/aug/12/best-portable-neck-handheld-fans-uk	\N	UK	2026-09-02 10:09:33	2026-09-03 23:32:49.268	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{gadgets,summer,"life and style",travel,"health & wellbeing"}
