cmtm5udq5005mzlilfopasvd7	Caregiver creep: how helping elderly relatives can snowball into a full-time job	Assisting an ageing loved one can start with small tasks but may lead to full-time caregiving that requires more support\nHelping an ageing parent or loved one often begins with easy-to-field requests: Pick up a prescription. Drive them to a doctor’s appointment. Grab groceries.\nHowever, light tasks – just “helping out” here and there – may eventually snowball into the avalanche of full-time caregiving, a phenomenon becoming known as “caregiver creep”. Like “caregiver burnout”, it’s not a formal term, but there is growing awareness about this slow build among healthcare providers and experts.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/wellness/2026/aug/31/caregiver-creep-caring-elderly-relatives	\N	UK	2026-08-31 16:00:30	2026-09-03 23:32:49.277	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"well actually",ageing,carers,health,"social care",society}
cmtm5udq0005izlilecbisdnq	Jam season is here - bottle that sunshine now to brighten up the winter	September’s preserving season is a race to capture the heady flavours of summer fruit\nSign up here for our weekly food newsletter, Feast\nThe ideal time to make lemonade, when lemons are juicy and plentiful and cheap, is in the dead of winter, when the last thing you want is a cooling glass of lemonade. Similarly, jam-making is best done on a grey November afternoon, when the windows are steamed up and the kitchen is the warmest place in the house. Winter is exactly when you want to be standing over a great pot of bubbling fruit and boiling sugar, but much less so in summer, which is when all the good fruit is in season. From May until mid-October, our fruit supplier bills spike exponentially as boxes and boxes of seasonal fruit in its absolute prime make their way to our kitchen for a jam session, and the practice of turning them into a sweet spread is part profession, part obsession. Jam is very much our jam.\nEarly in the season, we can just about cope. The soft fruits start to come in when the weather is still behaving itself, and we love giving them a little seasonal accent – elderflower comes with the last rhubarb, as well as with the first strawberries and apricots, and it pairs wonderfully with all of them (if you are starting there, Felicity Cloake has a very thorough guide to the perfect strawberry jam). Cherries, meanwhile, go with just about anything, while young bay leaves often find themselves heading into the blackcurrant pot. Fennel fronds and peaches are another favourite, and we make some whenever we can get those two at the same moment. It is an amazing combination: sweet, perfumed and just a little bit green.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/food/2026/sep/01/feast-newsletter-itamar-srulovich-sarit-packer-jam-and-preserving	\N	UK	2026-09-03 11:35:34	2026-09-03 23:32:49.272	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udq1005jzlil0sj0yi3h	Rachel Roddy’s summer minestrone with pesto – recipe | A kitchen in Rome	A fresh, seasonal vegetable soup that can be made in advance and only improves in texture and flavour over time\nNow that I am an accomplished sourdough maker, soon to start my own micro-bakery and YouTube channel … Only joking! Now that I can make half-decent, variable loaves of sourdough, I have become more aware of how the temperature in the kitchen affects food. Not that I was completely unaware, of course – I have curdled enough milk and neglected enough eggs – but feeding, then watching the sourdough starter in a jar, and then the dough itself, seeing how the process takes hours in winter, speeds up in spring, then takes on a foaming, bubbling and at times terrifying life of its own in summer, are lessons in living microorganisms and fermentation.\nIt is a shame, then, that I didn’t give the same attention to the pan of summer minestrone I had felt so pleased with myself for cooking, cutting the courgettes carefully so they looked like Trivial Pursuit scoring wedges, while drinking tea and listening for the bottle bank lorry to drop the glass into its box, and before the sun (or anyone else) was up. Even though the combined taste of minestrone and milky Yorkshire tea is interesting, I knew it had turned out particularly well. I put on the lid, left the pan at the back of the stove and went out.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/food/2026/sep/03/summer-minestrone-with-pesto-recipe-rachel-roddy	\N	UK	2026-09-03 05:00:44	2026-09-03 23:32:49.273	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
