cmtm5udpz005hzliliyrqf4wm	Are there any drinkable canned cocktails?	Palatable premix cocktails – well, ones that adults like, at least – are far and few between\nFor somebody whose children recently learned how to crack open a tinnie, this week’s tasting was a perilous one. Cans covered in seductive, brightly coloured pictures of parasols, palm trees and their favourite fruits filled the kitchen, and both the seven- and the five-year-old tried to take a swig. Premix cocktails are disproportionately marketed to young drinkers – gateway booze, if you will, as the likes of Bacardi Breezer pineapple was for me – and often have a one-note, cloying taste. Absolut’s passionfruit martini and Malibu’s piña colada are cases in point: both have sugary, synthetic fruit flavours that mask, or even eliminate, the taste of the base spirits. Is it any wonder that, in 2024, Britain had the worst rate of child alcohol consumption in the world? These drinks make it easy to forget that you’re even drinking alcohol.\nPerhaps more than any other theme, writing about premix cocktails has made me confront the very question of why we drink alcohol. Take me as an example. My Bacardi Breezer years were undoubtedly a question of booze for function; it loosened my teenage inhibitions and I had a laugh. The fact that this so-called “alcopop” was so saccharine chimed with my childish predilection for sweetness, and conveniently masked the flavour of the rum, which may at the time have offended my uninitiated palate. Twenty-five years on, and my palate is a different place, and I welcome the notes and nuances of a well-mixed drink. I now drink alcohol to savour it, and I like what I’m drinking to give me pause; it doesn’t need to be hard work, but I do like it to make me think.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/food/2026/sep/03/premix-cocktail-cans-mina-holland	\N	UK	2026-09-03 12:00:29	2026-09-03 23:32:49.271	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{food,cocktails,spirits,fruit,"mexican food and drink","italian food and drink"}
cmtm5udq3005lzlil64n8g5g7	I thought I was too broken for sex. Here’s how I found pleasure again	I was forced to examine the idea of sexual pleasure after a tumor was removed from my spinal cord\nTen years ago, my right elbow started to hurt. The internet suggested I had tennis elbow, so I stopped playing tennis. Once the outer elbow pain moved inward, I changed my self-diagnosis to golfer’s elbow. I stopped lifting weights, competitive dragon-boating and bouldering.\nFor good measure, I consulted an occupational therapist about my nightly bullet-vibrator routine after reading that golfer’s elbow is common among construction workers who operate heavy-duty vibrating machinery. He listened closely and said he’d note VRI on my patient chart. I asked what VRI meant, eager to end my diagnostic journey. “Vibrator-related injury,” he responded. We busted out laughing.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/wellness/2026/sep/02/sex-pleasure-disability	\N	UK	2026-09-02 16:00:28	2026-09-03 23:32:49.275	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"well actually",sex,disability,"life and style"}
cmtm5udq6005nzlilcmb00c6s	The pet I’ll never forget: Jerry, the king of dogs, who taught me how to live	After a burglary, my parents decided we needed extra security. Jerry was the answer – and this hungry, handsome hound helped us all in countless ways\nJerry came into our lives because a burglar had broken in. My mum had woken in the middle of the night to find an intruder in our kitchen. He scarpered, but the psychological damage was done. Two days later, my dad came home with the cutest little rescue puppy, with traces of beagle and labrador in him. He was two months old, had a black, brown and white coat, and big, bashful eyes. We called him Jerry because we had a cat called Tom. “I’m not sure how he’ll protect us,” I said. “He will,” my dad replied.\nJerry was very laid-back and Zen. He became part of the family straight away. He also became the therapy dog we hadn’t realised we needed. With two teenagers in the house (me and my older brother) and a hyperactive younger brother, someone was always in a strop and needing to let off steam by taking him out for a walk. Each time, Jerry obliged. I’d bore him with my latest teen-romance trauma; he was a very good listener. Sometimes, he’d have five walks a day, with both of my parents taking their turn. He certainly got his steps in.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2026/aug/31/the-pet-ill-never-forget-jerry-the-king-of-dogs-who-taught-me-how-to-live	\N	UK	2026-08-31 10:00:22	2026-09-03 23:32:49.278	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
