cmtm5udqd005uzlilzmlwkzqc	Is it true that … mental health is affected by inflammation?	Studies have found that anti-inflammatory drugs can improve your mood. But what does that mean for conditions such as depression and anxiety?\nInflammation is the immune system’s response to injury or infection. If you cut yourself and the area becomes red and swollen, that’s inflammation: immune cells are rushing to fight germs and repair tissue.\nBut inflammation can also occur in the body during illnesses, and a low level of inflammation can arise from obesity or ageing. “A marker of that inflammation tends to be elevated cytokines in people’s blood,” says Prof Daniel M Davis, head of life sciences at Imperial College London and author of Immune Health: A Myth-Busting Guide. “They’re the molecules immune cells use to communicate with each other.”\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2026/aug/31/is-it-true-that-mental-health-is-affected-by-inflammation	\N	UK	2026-08-31 07:00:18	2026-09-03 23:32:49.285	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"life and style","mental health",health}
