cmtm5udqt0067zlilfct8q247	A moment that changed me: I had a panic attack on public transport – and it led me to a horrifying secret	For a decade I lived with a fear so bad that I once tried to climb out of a tube carriage while it was in motion. Then a hypnotherapist told me to ask my parents one very important question\nIn 2000, I had been living in London for just over a decade and working at EMI Records for five years. I had moved to Brixton from Surrey – where space and peace were generally abundant – in the late 80s, and I’d noticed I was becoming increasingly anxious and claustrophobic, particularly on public transport.\nAs things got progressively worse, I had a string of distressing panic attacks. Every time, it felt like the world around me was closing in. Then, in July 2003, during my daily commute between Brixton and the South Bank, I had a panic attack so intense I tried to climb out of the tube carriage doors. Thankfully, other passengers held me back until we arrived at the next platform – but I knew the problem was no longer something I could ignore.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2026/sep/02/moment-that-changed-me-panic-attack-public-transport-horrifying-secret	\N	UK	2026-09-02 05:45:24	2026-09-03 23:32:49.301	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtm5udqo0064zlils2xybllz	My search for the perfect Polish dumplings in Gdańsk	Pierogi are a religion in Poland, but I’d never been a believer. Could a recce in the charming Baltic city convert me?\nI used to live in Poland. I worked in a fish-and-chip shop in the western city of Poznań, boning cod and peeling potatoes for £1.50 an hour. With the exception of that job, I enjoyed everything about my time in the country, not least the food. I liked the chicken broth with noodles, the slippery herring, the knuckles of pork the size of basketballs, the cold beetroot soup that beats gazpacho hands down.\nOne thing I was less delighted by were Polish dumplings – pierogi. It wasn’t that they weren’t nice – they were – it was more that they didn’t seem to warrant the extreme levels of affection and esteem Poles often bestow on the doughy parcels. By being ambivalent about them, I was going against a well-established grain.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/aug/31/perfect-pierogi-polish-dumplings-gdansk-poland	\N	UK	2026-08-31 06:00:19	2026-09-03 23:32:49.296	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"poland holidays","food and drink","cultural trips","europe holidays",travel,restaurants,restaurants,food}
