cmtnajsrz002o33itd1x8lmjt	The Guardian view on Reform’s funding scandal: Nigel Farage has questions to answer | Editorial	An undercover investigation raises issues around foreign money, political transparency and the rightwing populist party’s fitness for power\nReform UK’s conference was to open showcasing a party ready for government. It did so with two top aides removed after an investigation aired on Channel 4, its finances under police scrutiny and seven in 10 voters, polls suggest, regarding its leader, Nigel Farage, as sleazy – double the number who thought the same of Andy Burnham. Reform, a rightwing populist party, says it will take power back from a corrupt establishment. The latest scandal raises a question: does Reform oppose the influence of big money – or just who benefits from it?\nUndercover reporters from Verbatim Investigations, founded by journalists from the US Center for Climate Reporting, pretended to be a wealthy American father and his UK-based son who wanted to donate to Reform. In the UK, foreign donations are banned from politics. But that allegedly was ignored. Verbatim’s team filmed their meetings with James Orr, a Cambridge academic specialising in ethics, and Reform’s head of policy, as well as with Dan Jukes, Mr Farage’s close aide. Both have been “removed” from their posts.\nDo you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our letters section, please click here.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/04/the-guardian-view-on-reforms-funding-scandal-nigel-farage-has-questions-to-answer	\N	UK	2026-09-04 16:52:08	2026-09-04 18:32:19.823	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"reform uk","nigel farage","party funding",politics}
cmtnajss1002p33itmnwgc1wu	The Guardian view on music and drama education: reversing elitism starts at school | Editorial	The number of students taking creative subjects at A-level and GCSE is at a record low. The arts must not become the preserve of the most privileged\nHow many of the young people who attended Reading and Leeds festival or a Prom this summer will be picking up a guitar or clarinet when they return to school? New figures suggest not many. The number of students taking A-level music is at a record low: under 5,000, a drop of nearly half from 15 years ago. “We are facing the worst-case scenario prospect of A-level music disappearing from English state schools entirely,” warned Deborah Annetts, the chief executive of the Independent Society of Musicians. Music GCSE entries have also fallen. It has the widest attainment gap for disadvantaged students of any subject. Private violin lessons are beyond the purse strings of most parents.\nThe statistics for drama are similarly bleak. A-level drama entries have almost halved in 10 years, with by far the biggest drop in state schools. Forty per cent of state schools no longer even offer music or drama GCSEs. Leading universities have been forced to close music departments and arts courses. The number of teachers training in creative subjects has also declined sharply over the past 15 years. It is no coincidence that the Ebacc, which prioritised core academic subjects at the expense of the arts, was introduced by the Conservatives in 2010. No wonder the worlds of music, theatre and television have become so privileged.\nDo you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our letters section, please click here.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/04/the-guardian-view-on-music-and-drama-education-reversing-elitism-starts-at-school	\N	UK	2026-09-04 16:49:26	2026-09-0