cmtnajstm003p33ittwu93wsl	The Story review – thrilling drama of race, truth and the pursuit of headlines	National Theatre, London                                    \nLetitia Wright plays the ambitious young reporter seduced into cutting corners with contentious reporting in a brilliantly kinetic production\nTracey Scott Wilson’s play follows in the fine tradition of the fast-paced newsroom drama, beginning with the cold-blooded murder of a white schoolteacher by an unknown Black assailant as his pregnant wife watches. Cue moral panic, rolling news and the entry of a fiery young journalist determined to solve the case.\nThat is where the Watergate-style investigative blueprint warps because Yvonne (Letitia Wright, of Black Panther fame) is no simple hero in pursuit of truth. She is an ambitious Black woman, assigned to an enclave of a newspaper reserved for Black journalists. Her imperious editor, Pat (Lorraine Toussaint) is after positive news stories on the Black community while her white editor boyfriend, Jeff (Wilf Scolding) promises her a bigger gig if she proves herself worthy.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/stage/2026/sep/04/the-story-review-letitia-wright-national-theatre-london	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:46:19	2026-09-04 18:32:19.882	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{stage,theatre,"letitia wright","national theatre",culture,race,media}
cmtnajstp003s33itgcap85h9	‘Many of us in Israel live in a state of shock at the savagery’: veteran director Amos Gitai returns to Gaza	The writer-director once called Israel’s ‘one-man New Wave’ is in Venice with The Road to Jericho, a new and poetic attempt to highlight the plight of those in the occupied West Bank\nThe Palestinian village of Khan al-Ahmar sits off dust-blown Highway-1, between Jerusalem and Jericho in the Israeli-occupied West Bank. At the village centre is the “school of tires”, a fragile construction of rubber, wood and adobe which is attended by around 300 Bedouin boys and girls. The Israeli authorities denied the villagers an official building permit and ruled that the entire settlement is illegal. For the past eight years the school has been operating under threat of imminent demolition, a symbol of resistance to widespread settler violence.\nIt is only international pressure, says the veteran Israeli writer-director Amos Gitai, that has kept the place upright and still open. “The school has become a litmus paper. It is a test of how far the international community will go [to protect the rights of the people of Khan al-Ahmar]. For once, on this issue, Europe stood up and said no.” As for the future, who can tell?\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/israel-director-amos-gitai-gaza-the-road-to-jericho-venice	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:55:19	2026-09-04 18:32:19.885	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtnajstr003t33itnc68u53o	‘If you can’t understand what sex is, it’s irrelevant if you seem to be enjoying it’: how Queen at Sea upended the dementia drama	Lance Hammer’s first film in 18 years is a heart-wrenching masterpiece about family, mental decline and sexual consent. He and supporting actor Juliette Binoche discuss how they brought unabashed realism to a condition rarely seen on screen\nThe first time we see Martin and Leslie, the elderly couple at the centre of Lance Hammer’s wrenching new film, Queen at Sea, they are walking together up a set of stone steps: one of their regular outings, we assume, in days filled with pottering and quiet companionship. They have their backs to us, and we can’t see their destination beyond a bleached sky that fills the top half of the screen. Symbolically, it may be deemed a bit on the nose: two people near the end of their lives, moving gently towards the white light of oblivion. There’s a whiff of sentimentality about it.\nBut any doubts about this story’s willingness to confront difficult truths are blasted away by the next scene. Leslie’s daughter, Amanda, played by Juliette Binoche, pays a visit to the couple’s house in drizzly, middle-class north London, her teenage daughter in tow. Unable to find her mother and stepfather, she arrives at the bedroom door. Opening it, she finds Martin (Tom Courtenay) on top of Leslie (Anna Calder-Marshall), who lies impassively beneath him, the mask of dementia obvious on her face.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/if-you-cant-understand-what-sex-is-its-irrelevant-if-you-seem-to-be-enjoying-it-how-queen-at-sea-upended-the-dementia-drama	\N	UK	2026-09-04 07:00:00	2026-09-04 18:32:19.887	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{}
cmtnajsto003r33it7addb8jz	Gal Gadot’s Wonder Woman was one of DC’s few successes. It’s a mystery why she got the chop	James Gunn and Peter Safran’s regime had good reasons to ditch Ben Affleck’s Batman and Henry Cavill’s Superman. Other choices have been much harder to fathom\nThere was never going to be a completely painless way to break up the old DC gang. Warner Bros’ DC Extended Universe probably needed to be ruthlessly ripped apart in order to create space for James Gunn and Peter Safran’s brand-new DCU. We almost certainly did need a clean break from the likes of Ben Affleck (Batman) and Henry Cavill (Superman), if only to persuade audiences that this really was a proper reboot. The question, as Gal Gadot may have been pondering this week, is quite why some of the old universe’s supporting players were smuggled safely into the new continuity while others were left sprawling amid the ruins.\nSpeaking on the Happy Sad Confused podcast, Gadot, who starred in two solo Wonder Woman movies for director Patty Jenkins, revealed that nobody at DC Studios ever formally told her she had been dropped. One minute, Gadot says, she was being assured by Gunn and Safran that they would develop a third instalment with her; the next, she was looking on in mounting alarm as the entire infrastructure of the previous DC continuum was blown to smithereens. “They were like, ‘We’re going to develop it with you, the third one,’” she said. “But I saw the way everybody else was handled, right? And I, you know – the Henry [Cavill] situation was unfortunate. I was in the middle of that. The Henry situation was not handled elegantly, to say the least. Same with Patty – it was not handled elegantly.”\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/gal-gadot-wonder-woman-was-one-of-dcs-few-successes-its-a-mystery-why-she-got-the-chop	\N	UK	2026-09-04 10:19:34	2026-09-04 18:32:19.884	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{film,"wonder woman","gal gadot","dc comics","james gunn","henry cavill","patty jenkins",culture}
cmtnajsts003u33itjocc6362	‘Men calling us woke need our show more than most’: punks Piss on finding strength in visceral confrontations of sexual violence	The Vancouver band earned a cult fandom for their uncompromising gigs. As they ready their brutal debut album, they discuss subverting audience expectations and the nightly toll of excavating trauma onstage\nYou’d be hard pressed to find a band with fewer airs and graces than Piss. First, there’s the unavoidable matter of their name, which they style in all caps, no nonsense; a word that scans as vulgar and combative but also somewhat facetious when displayed on their merch in loopy cursive, sometimes decorated with bows. The Vancouver quartet’s music is a merciless cyclone of hardcore, poetry, sound collage and performance art that revolves entirely around singer Taylor Zantingh’s experiences with sexual violence.\nOn stage at Eindhoven’s Hit the City festival in late August, the band greets the stacked crowd with a trigger warning before drummer Garreth Roberts, bassist Gavin Moya and guitarist Tyler Paterson launch into pulverising fits of noise-punk, broken up by audio samples of children laughing and feminist writer Andrea Dworkin talking about pornography. Zantingh hurls herself around, clutching at her stomach and flinging her pigtails before winding down to a nervous, twitching standstill. The intense set is finely tuned to present a narrative of gender-based violence not as entertainment, but as lived experience. They play it exactly the same every night, and have done for the last two years.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/04/piss-band-unforgettable-live-show-noise-punk-album	\N	UK	2026-09-04 12:00:06	2026-09-04 18:32:19.888	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{music,culture,punk,"rape and sexual assault","performance art"}
cmtnajstt003v33itu8kohywx	Berlin Philharmonic/ Petrenko/ Hadelich review – flawless musical beauty	Royal Albert Hall, London\nIn their second Proms concert, the Berlin Phil brought Beethoven’s Violin Concerto (with Augustin Hadelich the virtuosic and breathtaking soloist), and a detailed and compelling take on Scriabin’s third symphony\nIt’s the clarity that hits you first. The soft thuds of timpani that open Beethoven’s Violin Concerto landed from the back of the stage with the immediacy of taps on the shoulder, quietly urgent. The string sound was simultaneously luminous and translucent. Pizzicato bass-lines were shaped as if the main event and bubbled upwards through the rest of the texture. You’ll hear orchestral playing to match this on high-end studio recordings, of course, where the entire process can be geared to achieving such crystalline polish. And the BBC’s sound engineers can work miracles in real time on radio broadcasts of Proms performances. But to produce such lucidity live – particularly in the Royal Albert Hall, with its monumental bathroom acoustic – is another thing entirely.\nFor that, you need an orchestra of the calibre of the Berlin Philharmonic under its chief conductor Kirill Petrenko. But violinist Augustin Hadelich also served up his own brand of astonishingly flawless musical beauty as the soloist in the Beethoven. In the first movement, his rising octaves appeared to float out of the orchestra, his bowing inaudible – the kind of violin-playing, in fact, that appears to defy the laws of physics. The slow movement was a masterclass in bel canto, the third a biting, bouncing romp as both Hadelich and Petrenko pushed gleefully through the rondo theme. The apparent ease of Hadelich’s virtuosity – all his energy seemingly focused on phrasing and dialogue with the orchestra – was nothing short of breathtaking.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/04/berlin-philharmonic-kirill-petrenko-augustin-hadelich-prom-review-beethoven-scriabin	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:05:47	2026-09-04 18:32:19.889	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"proms 2026","classical music",music,culture,"berlin philharmonic","kirill petrenko"}
cmtnajstw003y33itv7vz5lgq	The best recent poetry – review roundup	Fetch by Colin Bramwell;  Girling by Eve Esfandiari-Denney; The Dead One, The Unconscious One, Thundering in Your Ear, Thriving Slumber by Jay Gao; Belling the Leopard by Clementine Ewokolo Burnley; The Matter by Padraig Regan\nFetch by Colin Bramwell (And Other Stories, £14.99)\nBramwell’s translation of Fernando Pessoa’s work into Scots won him considerable recognition: it took the Oxford Weidenfeld Translation prize and the Highland Book Prize. Between source and target language, there is always a gap; the translator endeavours within that gap to identify meaning and intention, and that is the difficult work of translation. Fetch, Bramwell’s own debut collection, lyrically thematises this gap in the poet-human’s condition: “I’m an instrument for something else / a digital piano that if you opened it up / you wouldn’t understand the wiring.” Fetch (in Celtic folklore, a doppelganger) is by turns solemnly tender, comically worldly, literarily devout. Here you’ll find deep engagement with Russian poets such as Osip Mandelstam and Anna Akhmatova, poems that fuse Scots and English, alongside filial memories and premonitions of fatherhood. “Death is certain, but back then I was too young for that shit.” For a debut, Bramwell’s poetics convey an uncanny sense of wisdom, patience and, eventually, peace.\nGirling by Eve Esfandiari-Denney (Fitzcarraldo, £12.99)\nBefore theme and subject, there is the pure, iridescent beauty of Esfandiari-Denney’s sentences; a singular voice that is alluring and sensuous, pleasurable to follow to whichever subject the poet chooses. The subjects are girlhood-cum-womanhood, the line between debilitating illness and delusional wellness (“thank god / you invested / in lion’s mane”), the flickering concept of free will for a species so programmed by genes: “I have wondered to what extent my cancer diagnosis is my mother’s, or my grandmother’s cell mutation, before my own … I look up from a cigarette mid-sentence…” The collection hybridises recognisable prose forms: the essay, the diary, the text message, the anecdote. These are interspliced with short-lined lyric poems that startle because of the bubbly voice from which they come: “I hope it’s ok to die. I can / become something / like actualised metaphor.” The magic of this collection is that it is never highfalutin, finding a way to make an ending like “DJ are you listening did you read to the end will you blow my voice a kiss” powerfully funny and moving.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/04/the-best-recent-poetry-review-roundup	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:00:05	2026-09-04 18:32:19.892	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{books,poetry,culture}
cmtnajstx003z33it027y78rq	The Golden Hours by Louisa Young review – this continuation of the Cazalet Chronicles is a total delight	Elizabeth Jane Howard’s niece has picked up the thread of her much-loved family saga series, taking the story into the 1960s\nElizabeth Jane Howard’s beloved family saga the Cazalet Chronicles spans three decades in the life of the upper-middle-class Cazalet family. The first four books, written in the 1990s, run from a bucolic prewar 1937 to a bruising 1947, while the fifth, 2013’s All Change, skips ahead, sitting lonely in 1956. The books are exquisite doll’s houses of feeling and object: both period detail and eternal emotion are rendered precisely and vigorously in Howard’s lovely prose. “Panoramic, expansive, intriguing as social history and generous in their storytelling,” Hilary Mantel wrote in the Guardian. The enormous cast of characters – four late-Victorian siblings, their parents, their children and partners and ex-partners and servants and friends and lovers and enemies and cars and houses – sprawl deliciously over the years. The family fortunes rise and fall. Marriages fail; deaths occur; babies are born; people fall in love. Things change; things stay the same.\nWritten a decade after the death of the original author, The Golden Hours by Louisa Young adds a sixth to this quintet. As with all Cazalet novels, to describe the plot would be impossible: it is simply that many people, whom we have come to love over the course of the previous novels, live their lives, now in the early 1960s.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/04/the-golden-hours-by-louisa-young-review-this-continuation-of-the-cazalet-chronicles-is-a-total-delight	\N	UK	2026-09-04 06:00:02	2026-09-04 18:32:19.893	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{fiction,books,"elizabeth jane howard"}
cmtnajsu3004033it3tkn6e9v	Kevin Powers: ‘I was ready to abandon Infinite Jest a thousand times’	The American author on falling in love with The Great Gatsby, being inspired by Dylan Thomas – and the memoir of a canoe trip in Texas he comes back to\nMy earliest reading memory\nThe deaths of Old Dan and Little Ann in Wilson Rawls’s Where the Red Fern Grows were etched into my being as a first grader in Chesterfield County, Virginia.\nMy favourite book growing up\nStephen King’s The Stand. I completely lost myself in the story. I don’t think I realised at the time what a rare gift it is to have that experience.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/04/kevin-powers-i-was-ready-to-abandon-infinite-jest-a-thousand-times	\N	UK	2026-09-04 09:00:02	2026-09-04 18:32:19.899	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{books,culture,poetry,fiction}
cmtnajsu5004133it5k3vovzt	Thieves target mail order sales as Pokémon card prices soar	As one card recently sold for £12m, opportunistic thefts have surged – envelopes are being opened en route, costing small businesses thousands\nPokémon’s success over the last 30 years has seen it grow from a much-loved Game Boy classic, to a world-dominating brand with TV shows, spin-off games, toys and even a theme park. The trading card game is a huge part of that, with more than 85bn cards printed over its lifetime, and the appetite for them shows no signs of slowing down. Packs get snapped up after release so quickly it’s now almost impossible to find them in shops, and a booming resale market is driving the hobby into murky waters.\nOne card recently sold for an eye-watering £12m, and an increasing number of people have gotten into the collecting business on the hunt for huge profits, leading to a rise in thefts targeting card shops. More recently though, some small businesses have reported that their sales have been hit by thieves targeting envelopes within Royal Mail, leading to thousands in lost revenue.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/games/2026/sep/04/thieves-target-mail-order-sales-as-pokemon-card-prices-soar	\N	UK	2026-09-04 09:35:03	2026-09-04 18:32:19.901	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{games,pokémon,crime,culture,"royal mail","uk news"}
