cmtnajsr3002833itsxeibbjg	UN votes to adopt new world map that shows Africa’s true scale	Togo-sponsored resolution called to phase out traditional map that depicts continent as similar size to Greenland\nThe United Nations has voted to adopt a resolution for the world to formally phase out the traditional Mercator map of the globe in favour of one that more accurately displays Africa’s size.\nIn a session at its headquarters in New York, the UN general assembly officially retired the 16th-century Mercator projection in favour of one based on the more accurate 2018 Equal Earth design.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/04/un-vote-world-map-mercator-equal-earth-africa	\N	UK	2026-09-04 16:49:29	2026-09-04 18:32:19.791	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{africa,"united nations",togo,"african union","world news"}
cmtnajspt001w33it5471zhlj	Chat to strangers and let your kids be bored: how to embrace a ‘slow September’ – and enjoy life more	The pressure to self-optimise at the start of the new school year can lead to disappointment and burnout. Instead, here are seven ways to slow things down and reap the benefits\nIt is a hangover from our school days that September can feel like a time for getting back to the grind. As in January, there is a sense that we should return as better workers, partners, friends and parents. This sense is even stronger now a culture of optimisation – has taken over – evidenced by the fad for adding “maxx” to everything. Yet that can lead to disappointment and burnout, which Mental Health UK reports up to 91% of adults were at risk from over a recent 12-month period.\nOne antidote to this drive is to embrace a slower way of living. Rather than always rushing from one activity to the next, we could be enjoying life.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2026/sep/04/chat-strangers-kids-bored-embrace-slow-september-enjoy-life-more	\N	UK	2026-09-04 14:00:10	2026-09-04 18:32:19.745	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"health & wellbeing","life and style","work-life balance","mental health","parents and parenting",family,health,"work & careers"}
cmtnajsqh002033ittelv2mfp	‘Like a tired boxer, battered by applause’: as Andrew Scott’s biopic premieres, Richard Eyre remembers Ian Charleson’s superb, near-death Hamlet	In 1989, the Chariots of Fire actor took on the incredible task of playing Shakespeare’s prince while terminally ill from Aids. This weekend sees the world premiere of Elsinore, a new film about the episode, itself heavily-tipped for awards\nIan Charleson was one of the finest actors of his generation and an important figure in the fight for gay rights. Yet the extraordinary circumstances of his final performance as Hamlet, just months before his death, are less familiar.\nNow, a new film is bringing that haunting production back to life. Elsinore, directed by Simon Stone and starring Andrew Scott as the Scottish actor, will premiere at Telluride this weekend before opening the London film festival in October.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/like-a-tired-boxer-battered-by-applause-as-andrew-scotts-biopic-premieres-richard-eyre-remembers-ian-charlesons-superb-near-death-hamlet	\N	UK	2026-09-04 14:03:50	2026-09-04 18:32:19.769	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"telluride film festival",film,culture,stage,"richard eyre","andrew scott"}
cmtnajsqm002133ithssprgaf	‘I didn’t know if I’d ever have sex again’: inside Sharon Horgan’s excruciating post-divorce comedy	Horgan’s hilarious new show probes the perils of ageing and the horror of being single again – and it has sex scenes so wild you’ll be laughing for weeks. The creator and her co-stars open up about Youth\nWhen Sharon Horgan walked into HBO to pitch her new series, Youth, she basically just said “sandwich generation” to them. Alex, the protagonist, is squished between a teenage son and two elderly parents. That’s the classic; the rest of it – the recent divorce, the dating hell, the busy, creative professional life full of pricks – is the icing on the sandwich (if you like). Horgan had been incubating some of the characters for five years, but “honestly”, she tells me, “I said about three sentences and a week later, they said yes. I’ve never had that experience before.”\nYou might think you can imagine it, but Youth is unlike any portrait you’ve ever seen in the gallery of (for brevity) menopausal women doing everything for everyone. There’s an urgency and valour to it that is very much drawn from Horgan’s own experience. “Everything just changed for me when I hit my 50s,” she says. “I was very bamboozled but I really had a hunger to make time count.” Not every situation is lifted directly from her own life, post-divorce – she’s also borrowing from her friends, she emphasises – but in general terms, the pressures, the worries, the outrages, the surprise and the delight are all so exquisitely drawn that even if it’s not Horgan herself, you hope someone, somewhere, is living this in real life.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/sep/04/sharon-horgan-post-divorce-comedy-youth	\N	UK	2026-09-04 12:00:07	2026-09-04 18:32:19.774	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{television,"sharon horgan",drama,culture,"television & radio"}
