cmtnajsrn002i33ityny8o1b9	Andy Burnham presents his new government as more leftwing. In reality, it’s barely moved at all | Owen Jones	The prime minister talks the talk about reversing decades of privatisation and austerity, but we’re yet to see any policies to this end\nIf Starmerism sought to present the Labour government as more rightwing than it is, Burnhamism seeks to do the opposite.\nThe change in rhetoric is certainly stark. Unlike his sulking predecessor, the new prime minister offers both an analysis and a vision. British stagnation and decline, Andy Burnham correctly told the House of Commons, can be explained by a series of “wrong turns” taken from the 1980s onwards: the privatisation of economic power, the deindustrialisation of the country and the ruinous austerity that followed the 2008 crash.\nOwen Jones is a Guardian columnist\nDo you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our letters section, please click here.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/04/andy-burnham-more-leftwing-keir-starmer	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:57:11	2026-09-04 18:32:19.811	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"andy burnham",labour,politics,"uk news"}
cmtnajsrt002l33itt68o9vay	The Trump White House’s fetish for gamer culture has moved up a level with new arcade	A string of distractions on the US government’s own website invite you to catch pixelated migrants – showing gaming’s potential to be nastily weaponised\nThe Trump White House on Thursday released a horrible little propaganda arcade via its website, a collection of nasty riffs on popular games. Wrapped in the cutesy, nostalgic aesthetic of early-90s pixel art, these mindless fascist diversions are harmless-looking, but their intent is stomach-turning: this is the appropriation of play in service of propaganda. It’s just the latest in a series of attempts from Trump’s administration to use video game memes and imagery to further its messaging.\nHaving riffed on Halo and Pokémon in social media ads last year, inviting potential ICE recruits to “stop the flood” and “catch ’em all”, this time the administration has ripped off a series of recognisable games. Flappy Bill, perhaps the least gross, is a version of the old mobile hit Flappy Bird, except here you’re an eagle with a scroll in its claws. Supply Line could almost be an educational game about healthy food in schools, where you sort junk food off a conveyor belt destined for school lunches, except for some reason you play as a boxer who becomes more bloodied with every seed-oil containing, ultra-processed food item that you fail to punch into the garbage chute.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/games/2026/sep/04/trump-white-house-gamer-culture-arcade	\N	UK	2026-09-04 16:15:50	2026-09-04 18:32:19.817	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{games}
cmtnajsrv002m33itld9n3riq	When does rage help us, and when does it harm us? Ukraine has taught me the answers | Charlotte Higgins	Fury at Russia’s death and destruction has fuelled art, action and resistance. But eventually, rage’s fire is all-consuming\nTwice, I have heard Ukrainians perform Verdi’s Requiem. Once in Lviv, last Maundy Thursday, and once last month, under the great dome of St Paul’s Cathedral in London. In St Paul’s, performed by the Ukrainian Freedom Orchestra with the Philharmonia Chorus, the great Dies irae – “day of anger” – unfurled in an operatic gale of fury, reverberating so powerfully around Wren’s lofty spaces that the music felt like thunder echoing between mountain peaks.\nIn Lviv, where it was performed by a Ukrainian chorus and orchestra, the evening began with a call to remember the war dead. Lviv’s lay a mile up the road: 153 graves when I first visited in autumn 2022, and by this year more than 1,000. Ranks and ranks of soldiers killed in their 20s, 30s and 40s, each grave planted with flowers and plants, flags flying above. On that occasion, Dies irae – in the more sober acoustic of the Lviv Philharmonic Hall – seemed to contain the furious ache of grief. Certainly I have never heard a more wrathful day of anger.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/04/ukraine-artists-rage-requiem-resistance-literature-homer	\N	UK	2026-09-04 07:00:00	2026-09-04 18:32:19.819	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{culture,ukraine,"giuseppe verdi",poetry,homer,russia,europe,"world news"}
cmtnajsrx002n33it562m1w9c	Drones, teargas, riot squads: why has the UK gone to war against asylum seekers already fleeing conflict? | Diane Taylor	Burnham and Macron have committed to going further in the crackdown on French beaches. But little is being done to offer refugees safe alternative routes\nGarang*, 18, is one of thousands of refugees recently caught in the crossfire of French riot police, who threw teargas and beat them with batons when they boarded dinghies to travel to the UK. He is a victim of a war in his home of South Sudan – and one being fought on France’s northern shores.\nHe borrows a phone from anyone with credit who will let him try to call home, where his family is displaced. Garang doesn’t have enough money on the £10-a-week allowance from the Home Office to make a long-distance call. All he wants is to speak to his mum, to hear her voice, and let her know he is alive and safe in the UK despite the violence he has faced.\nDiane Taylor writes on human rights, racism and civil liberties\nDo you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our letters section, please click here.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/sep/04/drones-teargas-riot-squads-uk-war-asylum-seekers-burnham-macron	\N	UK	2026-09-04 10:21:42	2026-09-04 18:32:19.821	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"immigration and asylum","andy burnham",migration,"home office","shabana mahmood",refugees,france,"uk news",politics,"world news","emmanuel macron"}
cmtnajss8002s33it58dru3cl	Have a little fun scamming the scammers | Letters	Gary Calder responds to an article by a Guardian Money reader who got even by stringing the fraudsters along\nAnyone wishing to get their own back on bank-card scammers using the method described in your article (‘I’ve £80k in the account, here’s my pin’: turning the tables on the scammers, 29 August) might like to add a touch of realism to a made-up bank-card number by picking a known issuer identification number (the first six to eight digits) and ensure that the last digit conforms to the Luhn algorithm (it’s a check digit used to pick up errors in entering all the other digits). Further fun could be had by choosing an interesting postcode, such as SW1A 2AA (or 2AB – 10 and 11 Downing Street), SW1A 2JL (New Scotland Yard) or SL4 1NJ (Windsor Castle).\nGary Calder\nChandler’s Ford, Hampshire\n• Have an opinion on anything you’ve read in the Guardian today? Please email us your letter and it will be considered for publication in our letters section.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/money/2026/sep/04/have-a-little-fun-scamming-the-scammers	\N	UK	2026-09-04 16:54:12	2026-09-04 18:32:19.832	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{scams,"consumer affairs","nuisance calls",banking,crime,money,"uk news"}
