cmtnajstl003o33ittxa894j8	‘Why not try to be the best in the world, just for the fun of it?’ Art Garfunkel on a rich, restless life – and reconciling with Paul Simon	He taught maths and walked across Europe, trying to leave his demons behind. As he goes back on tour aged 84, the folk-pop legend explains how he came back to singing\nAs Art Garfunkel is keen to point out, “I am a singer – there, use that as the title of your article” – but he was never just a singer. “I’ve been an actor. I am a writer,” he continues. “I’ve walked across countries, taken freighters across oceans. I’ve read the dictionary. I’ve read two books a month for 60 years: Darwin, Herodotus, Balzac, Proust, Tolstoy. I’ve taught maths. I have two great kids and I’m a grandfather, but it always does seem to come back to the singing.”\nWhich is understandable, given that in Simon and Garfunkel (with songwriter Paul Simon) he sold more albums than any other pop duo in history. Garfunkel’s oft-called “angelic” tones powered classic chart-toppers including The Sound of Silence and Bridge Over Troubled Water, and he became a successful solo artist. However, until a year ago, he was hardly singing at all and hadn’t toured since 2019. “I found myself lying on a pillow in a dream state, thinking about the meaning of being alive,” he reveals. “What is my day? What am I supposed to do with myself?”\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/04/art-garfunkel-what-a-wonderful-world-tour-interview	\N	UK	2026-09-04 04:00:56	2026-09-04 18:32:19.881	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"pop and rock","art garfunkel","paul simon",music,culture,walking,film}
cmtnjhs37000rkhitd4fgujih	The Basics of Philosophy review – Paul Schrader back to his cool, cruel best	Venice film festival\nWhen a figure from his past turns up to a family funeral, a college professor faces an agonising dilemma that makes him question his own morality and whether he can change\nPaul Schrader is back with his most successful picture since First Reformed in 2018. It’s a stereotypical Schrader study of masculinity in some ways, about a man agonising over moral choice and ethical behaviour while – as so often in Schrader’s movies – reading and writing philosophy in an austerely furnished room. And yet there is nothing abstract about the issue he faces here. The film moves with a determined suspenseful tread, everything hangs together right up to the queasy final shot and Schrader flavours everything with a streak of melodrama and even a kind of gaunt black comedy. Karl Glusman’s cameo is surely the weirdest thing at Venice this year.\nThe scene is a blandly prestigious liberal arts college, presided over by a genial, grey-haired dean, played in cameo by Richard Gere. John Jorrisen, played by Jack Huston, is the star of the philosophy faculty, enjoying agreeable mid-career success, working on a book about Spinoza. He is cordially resigned to bored students showing up to his “The Basics Of Philosophy” course for what they assume will be easy extra credit, attracted by that undemanding word “basic”.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/the-basics-of-philosophy-review-paul-schrader-back-to-his-cool-cruel-best	\N	UK	2026-09-04 19:45:15	2026-09-04 22:42:42.163	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{film,"drama films","paul schrader","richard gere","venice film festival",culture,festivals}
cmtnajstn003q33itbmx81azc	15/18 A Place to Heal review – heartfelt docu-realist study of a French teen psychiatric facility	Venice film festival\nDirector Cédric Kahn combines compassion with coolly detailed forensic scrutiny in this genre-challenging film which puts a troubled love triangle at the centre of the story\nVeteran French director Cédric Kahn brings us a movie in a classic French vein: a docu-realist study of an institution, in this case a state psychiatric facility for teenagers between 15 and 18; with scenes in treatment suites, recreation areas, dorms and interview rooms. It’s something to compare perhaps with Laurent Cantet’s The Class, or Nicolas Philibert’s On the Adamant, or Canadian film-maker Denis Côte’s That Kind of Summer.\nKahn’s film is powerful and committed, although sometimes the acting comes close to shouty improv youth theatre. The tone is of course one of compassion, combined with coolly detailed forensic scrutiny, though audiences might be intrigued at Kahn’s use of Purcell’s Funeral Sentences on the soundtrack, music notably used by Kubrick in A Clockwork Orange, a film in which delinquent youth are treated more roughly. Kahn himself plays one of the senior medical staff, part of the gallery of authority figures – doctors, nurses, orderlies, therapists and a nervous intern – who are almost as affected by the institutional drama as the troubled young people themselves.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/1518-a-place-to-heal-review-heartfelt-docu-realist-study-of-a-french-teen-psychiatric-facility	\N	UK	2026-09-04 15:00:10	2026-09-04 18:32:19.883	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{film,"drama films",france,"venice film festival",culture,europe,festivals,"world news"}
cmtnajstu003w33it03vvlp7e	Add to playlist: the churning darkness of Barcelona producer Nueen and the week’s best new tracks	Nacho Pezzati’s new album Swerved taps into the moody ambience of the UK north-west underground, ratcheting a tangible and thrilling sense of unease\nFrom Barcelona, Spain\nRecommend if you like Space Afrika, Shackleton, Purelink\nUp next Swerved out now\nSwerved, by Barcelona-based producer Nueen (Nacho Pezzati) is a bassy, moody, darkly ambient album that travels through R&B, drill, cloud rap and dub without any obvious changes in its grey-black weather. But this is slowly churning music, on the brink of rot. By the 10th track, Copicat, the unease is tangible; after a slow, agonising ratcheting build of fanbelt hi-hats, the album’s only drop arrives with the weight and power of a cloudburst. It’s thrilling.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/04/add-to-playlist-the-churning-darkness-of-barcelona-producer-nueen-and-the-weeks-best-new-tracks	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:00:05	2026-09-04 18:32:19.89	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{music,culture}
cmtnajstv003x33itsi75kwft	Sairie: Beneath the Spreading Tree review – exquisite 70s-inflected folk heralds a move into autumn’s chill	(Self-released)\nSinger Emma Morton brings an indie-girl chorister’s brightness to this captivating debut album, on songs with hints of darkness that sound ages-old\nSairie’s debut album begins at this transitional time of year, its glistening psych-folk easing us out of the summer heat towards the coming darkness. In its Pentangle-like opener Song for the Winter, birds’ voices “climb higher”, hills “glow brighter” and waves “call out louder”, and the songs that follow are similarly saturated with colour and energy, creating an intensity that gradually reveals its perils.\nBased in Brighton, Sairie have released a stack of captivating singles and EPs since 2019, spanning folk, originals and modern covers; one great double A-side released earlier in the year had singer Emma Morton master the ballad May Carol and Françoise Hardy’s Tous Les Garçons et Les Filles. Her tone hooks an 80s indie-pop choirgirl-like brightness in tracks like Harder Than It Seemed, bolstered by Andy Thomas’s meaty bass, and makes you realise this style might have its roots in folk singers such as Maddy Pryor. It also channels the twisted beauty of Josephine Foster on songs such as The Breeze on the Meadow, where a wasp-like drone from Jon Griffin’s harmonium, Maria Marzaioli’s violin and Alice Eldridge’s cello amplifies hints of strangeness.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/music/2026/sep/04/sairie-beneath-the-spreading-tree-review	\N	UK	2026-09-04 08:00:02	2026-09-04 18:32:19.891	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"folk music",music,culture}
cmtm5udmi0039zlil8546u0fv	An Unexpected Life by Gloria Steinem review – a fitting tribute to a feminist icon	The activist’s memoir, due to be published this month, now stands as a posthumous testament to her remarkable life\nGloria Steinem was due to speak at a women’s lunch in New York at the end of this month and at more events in October, illustrating the central theme of An Unexpected Life, her new memoir, which, when it’s published a few weeks from now, will stand as posthumous testament to the 92-year-old icon: just keep going. So it is, after her death on Wednesday, that the book’s opening pages unfold with a deep, added poignancy and when Steinem expresses gratitude for a life that has been both happier and longer “than I ever imagined”, one is struck by the greater gratitude we probably owe her.\nAn Unexpected Life is a follow-up of sorts to My Life on the Road, the activist’s memoir of a decade ago, and as the tributes pour in, the book offers us an opportunity to go back over Steinem’s hardscrabble origins in Toledo, Ohio, and the roots of her activism. This is where it all happened, the formation of a woman who would come to define second wave feminism by combining a tough-as-they-come stance with something that looked a lot like serenity. Reading the first half of the book, one is reminded of Steinem’s original power and the improbability and iconoclasm of her journey, in which a childhood governed by poverty and dysfunction turned into a lifelong mission to resist the narrow templates laid out for her.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/03/an-unexpected-life-by-gloria-steinem-review-a-fitting-tribute-to-a-feminist-icon	\N	UK	2026-09-03 14:16:33	2026-09-03 23:32:49.146	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"autobiography and memoir",books,culture,"gloria steinem"}
cmtm5udpd004zzlilegs3oscs	‘Ruin and decay bring a chance of rebirth’: Hidetaka Miyazaki on uniting players in a damaged world	As FromSoftware’s new multiplayer game The Duskbloods nears release, the legendary game director behind Dark Souls talks connection, pessimism and of course, feet\nSince 2009’s Demon’s Souls, Japanese video game director Hidetaka Miyazaki has shown the world a different way to think about game design. His games defy convention with punishing fights, obscure rules, and unknowable, inhospitable worlds. The “Soulslike” genre – named after Demon’s Souls and its sequel Dark Souls – is now ubiquitous, influencing multimillion-sellers from God of War and Star Wars: Jedi to Hollow Knight. Miyazaki’s most recent success, 2022’s Elden Ring, which credited Game of Thrones author George RR Martin as a co-creator, sold over 30m copies and inspired Alex Garland to adapt it for the big screen at A24. It earned Miyazaki a spot on Time magazine’s “most influential” list, an accolade he shares with just one other game developer: Mario creator Shigeru Miyamoto.\nMiyazaki is now 51 and has been FromSoftware’s president since 2014, elevated from game designer to CEO after the success of the Souls series. He has a cherubic face that could pass for 35. He rarely speaks publicly, and never on camera. When he does speak, he answers questions like one of his characters – softly, sadly, his head bowed, making eye-contact only when I am speaking. Outside the studio where we speak is the sprawl of Tokyo’s Shinjuku, an ever-changing maze where tourists get lost and pop-up stores appear and vanish without a trace. Today, a J-pop band performs in the square as the cicadas scream from the trees. A gilded statue of Malania, one of Elden Ring’s toughest bosses, looms over this dimly lit conference room inside From’s headquarters, a bonfire checkpoint away from the chaos.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/games/2026/sep/03/ruin-and-decay-bring-a-chance-of-rebirth-hidetaka-miyazaki-on-uniting-players-in-a-damaged-world	\N	UK	2026-09-03 09:00:49	2026-09-03 23:32:49.249	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{games,"action games","role playing games",culture}
