cmtm5udph0051zliltjs06fm4	Star Wars Zero Company review – joyfully absurd heroics with a tight-knit team of misfits	PlayStation 5, Xbox Series S/X, PC; Bit Reactor\nSplit priorities between scripted and emergent story moments hamper an otherwise brilliant Star Wars-themed successor to XCOM\nEven if you’re unfamiliar with the term “emergent storytelling”, you’ve likely experienced some of the unscripted tales that can bubble up out of a game’s systems. Perhaps you’ve led a scheming dynasty in Crusader Kings, or watched a favourite hero succumb to madness in Darkest Dungeon. Maybe you’ve surrendered yourself to the infinite yarn-spinner that is Dwarf Fortress – or read about it from the mid-00s forums where users helped popularise the term “Let’s Play” by chronicling their games in descriptive posts. Or perhaps you’ve played Firaxis’ XCOM, the alien invasion tactics hit that several key members of Zero Company studio Bit Reactor had a hand in.\nZero Company hinges on a concept as simple as it is perfect: take the complementary class abilities, tough dice rolls, and permanent character deaths of XCOM, and house them in a sci-fi setting known for spaghetti western blaster duels, last stands and heroic sacrifices. As creative director Greg Foertsch puts it, “Star Wars is about loss.” Which losses you end up mourning in Zero Company depends on you.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/games/2026/sep/02/star-wars-zero-company-review-joyfully-absurd-heroics-with-a-tight-knit-team-of-misfits	\N	UK	2026-09-02 09:00:28	2026-09-03 23:32:49.253	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{games,culture,"strategy games","star wars",film,"playstation 5","xbox series s/x",pc}
cmtm5udpm0055zlilyind11gc	Present Laughter review – gender-switched Coward comedy dazzles in 70s New York	Theatre Royal Bath\nAntihero actor Garry becomes a magnificent new kind of beast as Gerri, played with blistering hilarity by Tracy-Ann Oberman\n\n\nThe reputation of Noël Coward’s plays has involved escaping restrictions: his recorded clipped, waspish tone in key roles and a perception that, at a time when gay men’s careers and lives were at risk, the dramatist could not be socially and emotionally truthful.\nSeven years ago, in an Old Vic revival of Coward’s 1942 off-stage comedy Present Laughter, Andrew Scott – as actor Garry Essendine, a gorgon of priapic self-importance – memorably banished the parodic playing style and probed forbidden subtext. Changing Joanna, one of Garry’s love interests, into Joe made characters bisexual or in sham marriages.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/stage/2026/sep/03/present-laughter-review-gender-switched-coward-comedy-dazzles-in-70s-new-york	\N	UK	2026-09-03 12:08:08	2026-09-03 23:32:49.258	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{stage,theatre,"theatre royal bath",culture,"noel coward"}
cmtnajsud004333itzu2oyhxt	Wallace’s dog Gromit was nearly a cat, reveals creator as Aardman turns 50	Nick Park began modelling the character as a feline, before finding it too difficult and changing him to a dog\nThe creator of Wallace and Gromit has revealed that the world’s most famous stop-motion animated dog was nearly a cat.\nAs Aardman Animations marks its 50th anniversary, Nick Park, who joined the studio in 1985, told the BBC he had originally modelled Gromit as a feline companion for Wallace.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2026/sep/04/wallace-gromit-nick-park-aardman-50-years	\N	UK	2026-09-04 11:36:46	2026-09-04 18:32:19.909	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"wallace & gromit",aardman,"animation on tv",television,film,"uk news"}
cmtnajsug004533itkxvgvpdu	Queer Edward II review – funny, touching time capsule of Derek Jarman at work	Venice film festival: Filmed during the making of his queer royal feature, while the director was living with HIV, the candid footage includes uproarious scenes at a Cliff Richard rehearsal\nThere could hardly be a more vivid time-capsule film about the publicly funded British arts scene in the early 1990s than this engrossing fly-on-the-wall documentary about Derek Jarman and his movie Edward II. Based on the play by Christopher Marlowe, it is about the 14th-century English king and his love affair with his court favourite, Piers Gaveston, with Steven Waddington and Andrew Tiernan as the leads and Tilda Swinton as Queen Isabella.\nThis film uses the tremendously funny and candid making-of footage shot at the time by Seamus McGarvey, who had worked as a camera assistant on earlier Jarman films War Requiem and The Garden, and whose distinguished career as a cinematographer lay ahead. Jarman emerges as an effortless leader with a beaming patrician authority, rather like David Lean or Richard Attenborough, the kind of chief whom subordinates instinctively wish to please while keeping from him the grubby problems that they themselves have to deal with, such as paying quarrelsome extras or handling tabloid press questions. Jarman had had his HIV diagnosis for five years at this stage, managing the situation with meds which McGarvey’s camera notices.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/sep/04/queer-edward-ii-review-funny-touching-time-capsule-of-derek-jarman-at-work	\N	UK	2026-09-04 12:00:07	2026-09-04 18:32:19.912	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{film,"documentary films","derek jarman","christopher marlowe","peter tatchell","lgbtq+ rights","venice film festival",culture,festivals,"uk news","world news"}
