cmtm5udq9005qzliloh6z8942	Freelancers are getting buried with ‘soulless’ AI slop cleanup: ‘It’s a shame we need to do it’	As more companies turn to AI, they’re hiring freelancers to clean up its mistakes rather than create original work\nLisa, a freelance graphic designer based in Spain, noticed a shift in her work after the release of ChatGPT in 2022. She went from receiving slow one-off jobs creating logos and packaging to an onslaught of requests asking her to fix versions that were generated by artificial intelligence – from sharpening fuzzy images for printing to turning flawed designs into usable files.\nBy 2025, Lisa, who asked not to be fully named to avoid solicitations, said 90% of her incoming logo and packaging design requests required cleaning up AI-generated content, work that accounted for 60% to 70% of her annual income. But the grind was exhausting. Clients lowballed her;' some AI-generated designs were so flawed that she had to recreate them from scratch and she worried about being complicit in AI-driven copyright infringement. By the end of the year, she began turning those jobs down.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/sep/02/ai-jobs-freelance-cleanup	\N	UK	2026-09-02 12:00:23	2026-09-03 23:32:49.281	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"ai (artificial intelligence)",technology,"art and design",chatgpt,openai}
cmtm5udlb002tzlilvczxu0oo	Do people really smell different as they get older? The truth behind ‘old person smell’	Experts say that the smell comes from a chemical compound called 2-nonenal, and it can show up much earlier than one expects\nLast week, my mom complained that she’s starting to smell “like an old person”.\nShe had grabbed her favorite sweater and draped it over herself at the movie theater. “It smelled like my grandmother,” she said, almost whispering. “I thought someone must have borrowed the jacket. But it was just me.”\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/wellness/2026/sep/03/old-person-smell	\N	UK	2026-09-03 16:00:42	2026-09-03 23:32:49.103	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"well actually",health,"life and style",ageing}
cmtnajsrf002e33it1thh39nv	For Gloria Steinem, fashion was about much more than aesthetics	In an era when feminists were stereotyped as unattractive, the pioneering writer’s style created a Trojan horse\n\nGloria Steinem obituary\n\nGloria Steinem: A life in pictures\n\nWhen people wanted to dismiss Gloria Steinem, they spoke about how she looked. Talking about the pioneering activist and journalist who turned feminism into a major civil rights movement, it might seem reductive to focus on her glasses.\nBut after Steinem – whose death at 92 was announced on Thursday – wore them on the cover of Newsweek in 1971 next to the cover-line The New Woman, the lightly tinted aviators – often worn over her hair – became an accidental form of self-branding that made her instantly recognisable. They were as indelibly linked to her legacy as Ruth Bader Ginsburg’s jabots or even Anna Wintour’s sunglasses. They also became one of her greatest weapons.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/04/gloria-steinem-fashion-feminism-aesthetics	\N	UK	2026-09-04 13:38:04	2026-09-04 18:32:19.803	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{feminism,women,"world news","life and style",fashion,newspapers,magazines,media,"newspapers & magazines"}
cmtm5udqg005xzlild5pyall9	Jess Cartner-Morley on fashion: swot up with these lessons in back-to-school dressing	September is the most fun time of year to shop, but it can also be dangerously seductive. Keep your wits about you with these tips\nI used to love back-to-school shopping when my children were little. The miniature highlighter pens, the name labels, those pop-out pencil cases from Smiggle, even the intergenerational wrangling over when the price of a water bottle becomes unreasonable. I love the ceremony of new beginnings. The curtain-raising drama of it all.\nLuckily for me, you don’t have to grow out of back-to-school shopping even though your children do, because in fashion September is considered the start of the new year. This is eminently sensible, because this time of year is a much better moment for a restart than January. The actual new year is dark and bleak and makes resolutions and projects feel intimidating rather than exciting. But right now, the evenings are still light, and while I’ve still got a bit of a tan pretty much anything feels possible.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2026/sep/02/jess-cartner-morley-fashion-back-to-school-dressing	\N	UK	2026-09-02 13:00:24	2026-09-03 23:32:49.288	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{fashion,"life and style"}
cmtm5udqj0060zlilbj6lgmzm	Dads and dudes: the two style clans that dominate menswear	The ‘dad’ look favours straight lines and muted colours, while ‘dudes’ prefer baggier clothes with adornments\nThere is a competition going on at the US Open this week and it’s not the one involving rackets and balls. Instead, leading men who are not entered into this year’s draw are serving looks rather than aces.\nTennis legend Roger Federer took to the court on Tuesday in navy blue polo shirt and shorts, navy blue sweatbands and navy blue socks. The cuts were trim and the top was tucked in. The young Spanish player Carlos Alcaraz wore a Nike vest, untucked and the colour of Ribena, maroon shorts, and trainers that looked like a sweetshop counter.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2026/aug/28/dads-and-dudes-the-two-style-clans-that-dominate-menswear	\N	UK	2026-08-28 18:03:37	2026-09-03 23:32:49.291	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{fashion,"life and style"}
cmtm5udql0062zlilia3y3rgw	Whisky galore – and now plenty more besides: exploring the Scottish island of Islay	Distilleries are the backbone of Islay’s economy and tourist trade. Thanks to their success, other businesses – from breweries and farms to restaurants and chocolatiers – are now thriving and adding to the island’s appeal\nThe Paps of Jura disappear into the mist as I pedal along the shores of Loch Indaal, and the rain cloud I’ve been racing finally catches up. I stash my hire bike at Port Charlotte youth hostel, quickly change and make it to Lochindaal Seafood Kitchen just before the downpour.\nI’m soon warmed, by the welcome then by a bowl of creamy fish chowder. “We’ve plenty of fresh seafood just in,” is all the temptation I need to order the seafood plate. It’s a bowl for one, but a hungry one, and soon I’m picking and cracking my way through piles of fat langoustines and crab claws, with hot whisky-butter scallops and plenty of bread and butter.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/sep/02/scotland-islay-whisky-food-and-drink-holiday-hebrides	\N	UK	2026-09-02 06:00:25	2026-09-03 23:32:49.293	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"scotland holidays","united kingdom holidays",whisky,travel,food,"europe holidays"}
cmtm5udqn0063zlilqdiu3knt	A walking tour of Andalucía’s historic white villages: ‘Nightingales serenade us as we wind along lush river valleys’	The famous pueblos blancos in southern Spain’s Sierra de Grazalema natural park teem with wildlife amid a limestone wonderland of peaks\n‘The wind is in from Africa / Last night I couldn’t sleep.” Joni Mitchell has ear-wormed her way into my head as my wife and I step out from Zahara de la Sierra into the mountains of Sierra de Grazalema natural park. The Africa bit probably comes from yesterday’s visit to Zahara’s castle museum. Here, high above the tiled terracotta mosaic of village rooftops, we learned how the recapture of this picturesque hill town was a turning point in the Reconquista – the military campaign of the 1400s that saw the Catholics retake southern Spain after seven centuries of Moorish control. As for sleep deprivation, I may just have overdone it on last night’s tapas.\nEither way, Africa feels present as we tramp through the countryside of Andalucía, if only in the wildlife. The painted lady butterflies drifting over the flower-spangled verges, the turtle doves purring from the olive groves and the booted eagle drifting overhead: all are summer visitors recently blown in from the great continent to our south.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/sep/01/walking-tour-andalucia-spain-historic-white-villages-pueblos-blancos	\N	UK	2026-09-01 06:00:47	2026-09-03 23:32:49.295	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"andalucia holidays","spain holidays","europe holidays",travel}
cmtnajsv9004f33itucv614n4	Will we have to choose different plants for hotter, drier summers?	Increasing heatwaves mean we need to rethink what we grow. The good news? You can still go cottagecore\nThe only spot-watering my garden has seen in recent months was done by the builder in a quiet moment. I hadn’t asked him to, but I think he saw the state of things and took pity on an acer I’d given up on. I explained I’d been using the drought as an, admittedly extreme, experiment to find which plants are resilient to multiple heatwaves, but said he was welcome to take the acer back to his well-irrigated garden.\nMy observations are that the roses hung in there. Not flowering, as I’ve seen others doing, but mine are probably younger. The wildflower meadow has been surprisingly floriferous; it’s been a good year for zinnias – although not good enough to make me like them. The volunteer buddleja that was hacked to the ground in April made an Oasis-worthy comeback. Euphorbias, meconopsis, sage, erigeron: all fine. Volunteer robinia saplings loved life. Verbascum coped. My son has been eating passion fruit straight from the vine.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2026/sep/04/gardens-plants-for-hotter-drier-summers	\N	UK	2026-09-04 10:00:04	2026-09-04 18:32:19.941	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"gardening advice",gardens,"life and style"}
cmtm5udkw002qzlils3jh9io3	The Bayeux tapestry: a stupefying, thrilling, horrifying masterpiece that shakes the earth	British Museum, London\nThe greatest battle ever depicted in art is here at last – and it hurtles along, making your heart pound with its sense of doom and destiny, culminating in fires and corpses on a battlefield full of pathos\nIt should not work. It should not even exist. Nothing about the Bayeux tapestry makes sense according to any norms of art: its creators couldn’t do proper portraits or pictorial depth or landscapes or any of the sophisticated things we associate with timeless masterpieces. When they depict a church, they have to write what it is: Ecclesia. But these artists – or who knows, perhaps she was a single artist – created something so stupefying and delightful, so abundant in visual thrills, horrors and humour, it beats almost everything else ever painted or carved – let alone embroidered on a strip of linen.\nAnd now here it is, in front of my eyes, close enough to touch if it wasn’t behind a glass screen of such high quality you never catch a reflection. The British Museum’s display of this wonder is perfect. It’s laid out in its case, the length of a gallery, which you reach after some archaeological foreplay involving armour, quotes from chronicles and info screens. Then you see the colours of the tapestry below you from a parapet – russets, greens, yellows – after which you simply start at the beginning and follow the story rightwards. Surrounded by darkness and picked out by finely adjusted lighting, the Bayeux tapestry shines. My only quibble is the ethereal soundtrack – shouldn’t there be drums, oliphants, troubadours?\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2026/sep/03/the-bayeux-tapestry-british-museum-london	\N	UK	2026-09-03 14:51:33	2026-09-03 23:32:49.088	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{art,"art and design",culture,"british museum","bayeux tapestry"}
cmtm5udqy006czlilt0y6u5so	Share your tributes and memories of Gloria Steinem	We would like to hear your tributes and memories of Gloria Steinem – whether you met her, or appreciated her work\nGloria Steinem, the American feminist and journalist whose activism helped push through greater rights for women around the world has died, aged 92.\nWe would like to hear your tributes and memories of Gloria Steinem – whether you met her, or appreciated her work.\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/sep/03/share-your-tributes-and-memories-of-gloria-steinem	\N	UK	2026-09-03 11:30:14	2026-09-03 23:32:49.306	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"gloria steinem",books,culture,feminism,"civil rights movement",women,"journalism books"}
cmtm5udr2006fzliln8lcw6od	Are you a Gen Z entrepreneur in the UK who has started your own business? We’d like to hear from you	We want to hear from young people who have chosen to start their own businesses rather than follow a traditional career path\nA growing number of Gen Z are starting their own businesses, with the number of Start Up Loans taken out by young founders rising by 132% over the past five years, according to analysis by the British Business Bank. Meanwhile, Hiscox analysis of Companies House data found nearly 400,000 Gen Z directors are now running UK-registered businesses.\nWe’d like to hear from people aged between 18 and 29 who have started a business about what inspired you to do it and how you got started. How is your business going? What have been the biggest challenges and rewards? Do you see yourself continuing to run your own business in the future?\n Continue reading...	https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/aug/21/are-you-a-gen-z-entrepreneur-who-has-started-your-own-business-wed-like-to-hear-from-you	\N	UK	2026-08-21 11:34:07	2026-09-03 23:32:49.31	cmtlwyprl000zhjil34s657op	{"small business",entrepreneurs,business}
